  "tour": {
    "title": "When Doubt Feels Like Failure",
    "intro": "You do not have to settle your fear before you are allowed to speak about it. This tour moves from a larger biblical picture of honest questioning to voices that distinguish doubt from rejection, then gives you words for mixed faith, grief, and the need for evidence. None of these clips treats uncertainty as something to hide or scold out of you.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:intro-wisdom-literature",
        "startSec": 1383,
        "endSec": 1460,
        "why": "Begin here for a wide-angle frame: the speakers say the wisdom writings are meant to lead us toward “an honest faith” that acknowledges doubts and questions we cannot fully answer. They also say that complexity does not automatically mean “God’s not real or faithful,” which gives you room to examine your fear without pretending life is simpler than it is.",
        "episodeTitle": "Intro to the Wisdom Literature",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/21fcf5ab-5d75-48ae-868c-19d7a7197c21/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=21fcf5ab-5d75-48ae-868c-19d7a7197c21",
        "durationSec": 1912,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "naked-bible:podcast:1702",
        "startSec": 3219,
        "endSec": 3387,
        "why": "This clip addresses your question most directly. The speaker carefully separates “having questions” and wondering what God is doing from “throwing your believing loyalty away altogether,” and then argues that even someone who has turned away can hear God’s invitation: “Come back.” It is a sustained answer to the fear that doubt has already made return impossible.",
        "episodeTitle": "Naked Bible 181: Hebrews 3",
        "sourceId": "naked-bible",
        "source": "Naked Bible Podcast",
        "audioUrl": "https://nakedbiblepodcast.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/10/NakedBible181_Hebrews3.mp3",
        "durationSec": 3898,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:1021fa12-com-1051a66c-213d-3b7f-9cb6-be4eaede94cb",
        "startSec": 1197,
        "endSec": 1304,
        "why": "Now listen for a prayer rather than a verdict. While explaining the father’s words in Mark 9, the teacher calls “I do believe, help my unbelief” an “incredible prayer” and says this is often where we find ourselves: belief and unbelief held together while asking Jesus for help.",
        "episodeTitle": "Mark 9:2-29",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/b6masi/mark_92-2996qgm.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1547,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "forty-minutes-ot:podcast:444302a2-d904-43b3-a21f-4c7fb0a30dd7",
        "startSec": 1369,
        "endSec": 1475,
        "why": "This gives doubt and pain a biblical form of speech. The hosts point out that Job, Jeremiah, the Psalms, and even Jesus’ cry from the cross do not read like an instruction manual for the correct emotional response. Their conclusion is striking: “lament rather than being a signal of the absence of faith is actually a sign that faith is very much present.”",
        "episodeTitle": "Job 2:11-3:26 (Episode 387)",
        "sourceId": "forty-minutes-ot",
        "source": "40 Minutes in the Old Testament",
        "audioUrl": "https://adbarker.com/stream/tfVE3XBxC9JCmb97VsZt7vpT/traffic.libsyn.com/secure/40minot/40_ep_387.mp3?dest-id=245197",
        "durationSec": 2435,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:d2454984-com-96e88155-08af-3240-94ab-4e13ecb0d2e9",
        "startSec": 1321,
        "endSec": 1475,
        "why": "End with Thomas, whose doubts are not erased by being mocked. The teacher says Thomas wants evidence because resurrection is not easy to believe, and emphasizes that Jesus “graciously humors him” by meeting his request. The clip leaves you with a picture of doubt being met by patient encounter, not shame, and with Thomas moving toward the confession “My Lord and my God.”",
        "episodeTitle": "John 20:1-31",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/z8yyjpv9fmtit6hd/john_201-3185o5y.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1726,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      }
    ],
    "closing": "Taken together, these voices do not promise that every question will receive a quick answer. They offer something gentler and more durable: questions can be brought into faith, mixed belief can become prayer, lament can still be directed toward God, and the frightened person who needs evidence is not beyond Jesus’ patience. For now, you might borrow the smallest prayer in the tour: “I do believe; help my unbelief.”",
    "totalSeconds": 612
  },
